WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.701
[tense music]

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:04.237
Nuri is one of the most
intensive concentrations

00:00:04.304 --> 00:00:05.605
of pyramids anywhere
in the world,

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.207
across any culture
and civilization.

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:09.509
[upbeat music]

00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:13.246
In Sudan, in fact, there are
more pyramids than in Egypt.

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:14.748
And this is
something that people

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:16.483
don't think of very often.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:22.055
NARRATOR: Kush and Egypt
have a long interwoven

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:24.791
history, one that
is just beginning

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:26.693
to be more fully understood.

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:31.731
FEMALE: Most of what we
know in the history books

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:34.634
about the kingdom of Kush
comes from the perspective

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:37.670
of the Egyptians, because
the Egyptians had writing.

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:41.374
And the Egyptians wrote
everything down, everything.

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:43.209
They pushed a phenomenal
amount of paper,

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:45.779
we would say in today's world.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:49.849
And we are fortunate to
have a lot of those records,

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:53.520
but those records frame
everyone outside of Egypt

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:59.225
as somehow not equal
to or lesser than.

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:01.261
History is written
by the victors, the 1%.

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:04.130
And archaeology tells the
story of the underdogs,

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:06.533
of the other 99%.

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:10.370
The best way to really verify or
disqualify a historical account

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:12.071
is to look for the
facts on the ground.

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:13.907
Sometimes it jives
with the history

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.141
books and sometimes it doesn't.

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:18.011
[tense music]

00:01:18.077 --> 00:01:19.546
NARRATOR: Archaeologist
Geoff Emberling

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:23.183
has excavated many important
Kush monuments, uncovering

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.251
its past brick by brick.

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:27.520
GEOFF EMBERLING: We know that
from the very first moment

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:31.491
that Kush appears in
history, around 2000 BC,

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:34.360
it was powerful.

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:37.564
It was so powerful that the
Egyptians, at that time,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.033
built a series of
fortresses along the Nile

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:45.472
to protect themselves against
the military power of Kush.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.740
NARRATOR: From its prime
position on the Nile,

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:51.177
the Kush Empire control
trade routes from the south

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:55.215
up to Egypt, transporting
ivory, leopard skins,

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:57.050
precious stones, and gold.

00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:59.619
GEOFF EMBERLING: They had the
connections with inner Africa

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.321
that could bring
these exotic products

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:05.091
all the way to Egypt and to
the wider Mediterranean world.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.593
FEMALE: They were
the go betweens

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:10.063
and they became very, very
rich and powerful off being

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:12.465
in that position.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:14.400
NARRATOR: Egypt
relied on Kushite gold

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:19.839
for their elaborate burials
and fierce Kushite warriors

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:22.809
to supplement Egyptian armies.

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:25.411
Eventually, the Kushites
gained so much power,

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:28.381
the Egyptians saw
them as a threat

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:32.318
and invaded their
neighbors to the south.

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:35.755
For the next 400 years, Kush
was controlled by Egypt.

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.293
In the beginning,
the Egyptians imposed

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.862
their gods and temples on Kush.

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:46.199
FEMALE: They were taking the
elites of Kushite society

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:49.235
and giving them
Egyptian educations.

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:50.737
NARRATOR: But the
Kushites, eventually,

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:53.239
became even more devout
spiritual followers

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:55.041
than their conquerors.

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:57.043
Even as the Egyptian
empire began

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:00.647
to lose strength and
withdraw in 1100 BC,

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:02.916
the Kushites continued
building their tombs

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:06.152
in the shapes of pyramids.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:08.154
The jewelry found
inside the burials

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:12.559
makes clear their
devotion to Egyptian gods.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:15.328
We know that by at
least, at the bare minimum,

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:18.932
by the 8th century BC,
Kush is on the rise.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:20.733
They have thrown
off the shackles

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.269
of their Egyptian
colonizers, but they do

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:25.405
retain some Egyptian elements.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:27.006
NARRATOR: For the
center of their kingdom,

00:03:27.073 --> 00:03:30.777
the Kushites took over a place
full of spiritual significance

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:35.181
to the Egyptians, a stunning
sandstone butte rising

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:38.751
high above the desert
landscape, Jebal

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:41.220
Barkal, or sacred mountain.

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:47.293
From here, the Kush kings were
able to control an increasingly

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.730
extensive territory.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:53.566
GEOFF EMBERLING: Ultimately,
that rise of power

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:56.603
led to the Kushites being
able to conquer all of Egypt,

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:59.772
and that's historically
just remarkable.

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.407
[upbeat music]

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:03.610
NARRATOR: For nearly
a hundred years,

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:08.414
a succession of five Kushite
kings ruled all of Egypt.

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:12.018
From Napata, their capital,
they controlled more area

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:14.721
than any other
Egyptian pharaohs,

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:20.560
stretching from modern day
Khartoum to the Mediterranean.

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:23.363
One of these Kushite
kings, a renowned warrior

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:28.601
named Taharqa, established
Nuri as a royal cemetery.

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.870
GEOFF EMBERLING: In many
ways Taharqa was the most

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:33.539
notable 25th dynasty king.

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:35.975
The cemetery that he
established at Nuri

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:40.480
became the royal burial ground
for Kush for over 300 years,

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:44.751
so he became the ancestor that
the succeeding kings of Kush

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.986
wanted to connect themselves to.

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:48.521
[tense music]

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:51.858
NARRATOR: Kings like Nastasen,
who Pierce Paul is hoping

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:55.194
may still lie inside his tomb.

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:58.398
I would like to find
evidence of Nastasen himself.

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:01.434
I'm not saying I want
to come up face-to-face

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:05.872
with him in the middle of the
third chamber in the dark,

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:06.939
but it'd be an experience.

